The `#loading` element never becomes hidden — meaning `loadFrame` never completes (prog never reaches 100, or the hide doesn't happen). Why? loadFrame: `prog += Math.random()*12; if(prog>100) prog=100; ... if(prog<100) requestAnimationFrame(loadFrame); else setTimeout(()=>{loading display none}, 300)`. 

The issue: when I replaced the script, maybe loadFrame is fine. But `requestAnimationFrame` in headless Chromium with the page not visible / backgrounded may throttle or not fire. Also, the page might be suspended. Actually the earlier capture (capture.sh) worked and showed the loading screen — but didn't wait for it to disappear.

Let me check: does loadFrame actually run? It's called at the bottom. rAF should fire. But maybe `Math.random()*12` increments slowly and rAF in headless is paused when tab not focused? Playwright page is focused though.

Wait, the real problem might be: the `.bar` inside `#loading` intercepts pointer events, so even after loading hides, the click targets... no, click on #btnStart failed because loading still visible (TimeoutError waiting for click, the element #btnStart is intercepted by #loading).

Let me make loading disappear reliably: instead of relying on loadFrame rAF, just hide it immediately or after a short fixed timeout. Better: change loadFrame to guarantee completion. Actually simplest robust fix: make #loading not block pointer events (set pointer-events:none on #loading) so clicks pass through to #splash. But then during loading the user could click start before ready — game not initialized? game.init() runs at script load, so it's ready. The loading is cosmetic. Let me set `#loading { pointer-events: none; }` and also make loadFrame complete faster by using larger increments + a timeout fallback.

Actually, cleanest: add a fallback that forces loading hidden after, say, 1500ms regardless. And set pointer-events:none on #loading so it never blocks interaction. Let me do both.

Let me edit the CSS #loading to add pointer-events:none, and add a fallback in loadFrame: a setTimeout at 2000ms to hide loading.

Let me update loadFrame: after the rAF loop, also set a timeout. Simplest: at start of loadFrame, if prog>=100 hide; and add `setTimeout(()=>{document.getElementById('loading').style.display='none'}, 1500)` unconditionally near the end of script.

Let me edit loadFrame to increment faster and add fallback timeout.

Current loadFrame:
```
let prog=0;
function loadFrame(){
  prog+=Math.random()*12; if(prog>100) prog=100;
  document.getElementById('loadFill').style.width = prog+'%';
  if(prog<100){ requestAnimationFrame(loadFrame); }
  else { setTimeout(()=>{ document.getElementById('loading').style.display='none'; }, 300); }
}
loadFrame();
```

Change to:
```
let prog=0;
function loadFrame(){
  prog+=Math.random()*14; if(prog>100) prog=100;
  document.getElementById('loadFill').style.width = prog+'%';
  if(prog<100){ requestAnimationFrame(loadFrame); }
  else { setTimeout(()=>{ document.getElementById('loading').style.display='none'; }, 200); }
}
loadFrame();
setTimeout(()=>{ document.getElementById('loading').style.display='none'; }, 2000);
```

And CSS #loading add pointer-events:none. Let me edit CSS #loading rule. Currently:
```
#loading{position:absolute;inset:0;background:#0a0c14;display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;justify-content:center;z-index:100;color:#dfe6f1}
```
Add `pointer-events:none;`.

Let me make these edits.

